## Why This Is Asked

This task tests the ability to decouple algorithmic filters from execution backends, enabling runtime backend swapping and new backend integration without touching core logic.

## Key Concepts

- Bridge pattern for decoupling abstraction (filters) from implementation (backends)
- Backend interface and concrete backends (CPU, GPU)
- Extensibility and testability with pluggable backends

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal illustrative code (not production-ready)
class Backend {
  apply(image, opSpec) { throw new Error('not implemented') }
}
class CPUBackend extends Backend {
  apply(image, opSpec) { /* CPU path */ return image }
}
class GPUBackend extends Backend {
  apply(image, opSpec) { /* GPU path, e.g., CUDA/Vulkan */ return image }
}
class Filter {
  constructor(opSpec) { this.opSpec = opSpec }
  render(image, backend) { return backend.apply(image, this.opSpec) }
}
class Pipeline {
  constructor() { this.filters = []; }
  setBackend(b) { this.backend = b }
  addFilter(f) { this.filters.push(f) }
  run(image) { let out = image; for (const f of this.filters) out = f.render(out, this.backend); return out }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add backends with different precision or memory constraints?
- How would you test backends in isolation and measure performance trade-offs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a runtime-extendable CLI core using a registry and plugin-like commands, focusing on isolation, concurrency, and robust error handling.

## Key Concepts
- Registry pattern
- Runtime extensibility
- Per-run isolation
- Concurrency with Promise.allSettled
- Plugin vs inline trade-offs

## Code Example
```typescript
export interface Command {
  name: string
  description: string
  execute(args: string[], ctx: RunContext): Promise<void>
}
export interface RunContext {
  env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv
}
export class CommandRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, () => Command>()
  register(name: string, factory: () => Command) { this.factories.set(name, factory) }
  unregister(name: string) { this.factories.delete(name) }
  create(name: string, ctx: RunContext): Command {
    const f = this.factories.get(name)
    if (!f) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`)
    return f()
  }
  async run(name: string, rawArgs: string[], ctx: RunContext) {
    const cmd = this.create(name, ctx)
    await cmd.execute(rawArgs, ctx)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you version commands and handle breaking changes?
- How to ensure command help output stays consistent across extensions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design systems that support runtime extensibility without downtime—a critical requirement for data platforms at companies like Plaid, Hugging Face, and Apple. It assesses understanding of how design patterns like Strategy, Factory, and Registry work together in live data ingestion systems.

## Key Concepts

- Runtime extensibility through a centralized registry
- Clear separation of concerns between validation logic, orchestration, and metadata-driven routing
- Zero-downtime deployment of new validation rules
- Dynamic validator selection and ordered execution

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core interfaces and registry
class IValidator {
  validate(record) {
    throw new Error('validate method must be implemented');
  }
}

class ValidationResult {
  constructor(isValid, errors = []) {
    this.isValid = isValid;
    this.errors = errors;
  }
}

class ValidatorRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.validators = new Map();
  }

  register(name, validatorClass) {
    this.validators.set(name, validatorClass);
  }

  create(name, config = {}) {
    const ValidatorClass = this.validators.get(name);
    if (!ValidatorClass) {
      throw new Error(`Validator '${name}' not found`);
    }
    return new ValidatorClass(config);
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Example validator implementation
class URLFormatValidator extends IValidator {
  validate(record) {
    const urlPattern = /^https?:\/\/.+/;
    if (record.url && !urlPattern.test(record.url)) {
      return new ValidationResult(false, ['Invalid URL format']);
    }
    return new ValidationResult(true);
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Runtime registration and usage
const registry = new ValidatorRegistry();
registry.register('url-format', URLFormatValidator);

// New validator can be registered at runtime
class EmailFormatValidator extends IValidator {
  validate(record) {
    const emailPattern = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
    if (record.email && !emailPattern.test(record.email)) {
      return new ValidationResult(false, ['Invalid email format']);
    }
    return new ValidationResult(true);
  }
}

// Register without broker restart
registry.register('email-format', EmailFormatValidator);
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to combine the Chain of Responsibility with a registry-driven plugin model, enabling runtime composition and reordering of middleware without touching route code.

## Key Concepts

- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry / Plugin architecture
- Dynamic composition and ordering
- Context mutation versus immutability

## Code Example

```typescript
type Context = { [k: string]: any };
type Next = () => Promise<void>;
type Middleware = (ctx: Context, next: Next) => Promise<void>;

class Registry {
  private store = new Map<string, Middleware[]>();
  register(name: string, mw: Middleware) {
    const arr = this.store.get(name) ?? [];
    arr.push(mw); this.store.set(name, arr);
  }
  get(name: string): Middleware[] | undefined {
    return this.store.get(name);
  }
}

function compose(list: Middleware[]): Middleware {
  return async (ctx, next) => {
    let i = -1;
    const dispatch = async (idx: number) => {
      if (idx < 0) return next();
      const mw = list[idx];
      if (!mw) return next();
      await mw(ctx, () => dispatch(idx + 1));
    };
    await dispatch(0);
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle middleware cancellation and timeouts without leaking resources?
- How would you test registry composition and ensure order guarantees across deployments?

